      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Summary and Overall Assessment of the Work:** This manuscript introduces a highly relevant and technically sophisticated methodology for characterizing quantum entanglement under realistic, non-ideal experimental conditions, marking a significant step forward in applied quantum physics. The conceptual framework presented is robust, and the initial experimental demonstration showcases the potential of this new measurement protocol in a complex physical environment.\n\n**Detailed Critique of Methodological Rigor and Statistical Analysis:** The statistical treatment requires substantial enhancement to meet the standards of this journal. Specifically, the derivation of the uncertainty bounds must be revisited; the current application of standard error propagation seems to neglect higher-order correlations inherent in the multi-parameter measurement scheme. I urge the authors to provide a more exhaustive sensitivity analysis demonstrating the robustness of their reported entanglement metrics against variations in detector efficiency and background noise levels.\n\n**In-Depth Examination of Environmental Effects and Decoherence Modeling:** The paper's current discussion of environmental coupling is notably underdeveloped. A deeper dive into the specific decoherence channels—such as amplitude damping versus phase damping—is necessary. The authors must explicitly connect the observed degradation of entanglement to a quantifiable master equation approach, rather than relying on phenomenological fitting parameters.\n\n**Assessment of Experimental Implementation and Hardware Constraints:** Beyond the statistical and theoretical aspects,",
